# Line endings are pinned here, not left to whoever clones.
#
# core.autocrlf is `true` on this machine. The committed blobs are clean LF
# today - checked, all 321 of them - but that is a property of the current
# setting, not of the repository. A clone onto a machine configured
# differently gets CRLF in every .sh, and on the server that is
# `/bin/bash^M: bad interpreter` or `$'\r': command not found` - a failure
# that names the shell and never the line ending. The whole point of a
# remote is that this tree gets cloned somewhere else.

* text=auto

# Anything an interpreter reads by its first line, or a Linux host executes.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.sql   text eol=lf
*.conf  text eol=lf
*.yml   text eol=lf
*.yaml  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# PowerShell is read by Windows; leave it as Windows writes it.
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Never touched.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.mp4   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.dump  binary
